    "h": "Homework from the lecture",
    "qs": [],
    "html": "<div class=\"rc-hw\"><b>The professor's prompt.</b> HW: build a model. (Cyclopentane: the two energy-minimum conformations.)</div>\n<p class=\"rc-src\">Original notes: <a class=\"rc-page\" href=\"../archive/pages/L1020-2.png\">10/20 page 2</a>.</p>\n<details class=\"rc-more rc-steps\"><summary>Try it on paper first. Then open the worked steps</summary><div class=\"rc-body\"><ol><li><b>Build the ring flat first.</b> Join five carbons into a ring, add two hydrogens to each, and press the ring flat on the table. Look down any C-C bond. The C-H bonds on the front carbon hide the C-H bonds on the back carbon: they are eclipsed. Count around the ring: 5 ring bonds, 2 eclipsed pairs on each, 10 eclipsing C-H/C-H interactions. The ring angles are 108°, so the model is not fighting you on angles.</li><li><b>Make the envelope.</b> Hold four carbons flat on the table and lift the fifth. Look down the two bonds that lead to the lifted carbon: the C-H bonds there are now staggered or close to it. Along the bond on the far side of the ring, between the two carbons opposite the flap, the C-H bonds are still eclipsed. Torsional strain is relieved, but not all of it.</li><li><b>Make the half-chair.</b> Hold three neighbouring carbons flat. Push one of the remaining two above that plane and the other below it. The ring is now twisted, and the remaining eclipsing is spread around the ring instead of being concentrated on one bond.</li><li><b>Notice what it cost.</b> In both shapes the ring angles are slightly smaller than 108°: you can feel the model resist a little. That is the meaning of the lecture's sentence: lots of torsional strain relieved, at the expense of angle strain.</li><li><b>Move the flap.</b> Push the flap down and lift its neighbour. The model passes through a half-chair and becomes a new envelope with almost no effort. The real molecule does this constantly.</li></ol><p><b>Answer.</b> Flat cyclopentane has 108° angles (very little angle strain) but 10 eclipsed C-H/C-H pairs. The envelope (four carbons in a plane, one out) and the half-chair (three in a plane, one above, one below) stagger many of those bonds. Both are energy minima: lots of torsional strain is relieved at the expense of a little angle strain.</p></div></details>"
