    "html": "<ul><li>Ring strain has two parts: angle strain (angles forced away from 109.5°) and torsional strain (eclipsed bonds).</li><li>Cyclopropane has 60° angles, bent bonds with poor overlap (C-C 65 kcal/mol against 88 in propane) and 6 eclipsed C-H pairs, and it cannot pucker.</li><li>Flat cyclobutane would have 90° angles and 8 eclipsed C-H pairs, so the real ring puckers to 88°, which relieves some torsional strain.</li><li>Flat cyclopentane would have 108° angles, very little angle strain, but 10 eclipsed C-H pairs, so it adopts the envelope and half-chair, relieving lots of torsional strain at the expense of angle strain.</li><li>Flat cyclohexane would have 120° angles and 12 eclipsed C-H pairs.</li><li>The chair of cyclohexane has no angle strain and no torsional strain: C-C-C is 111.5°, every carbon is tetrahedral and every C-H bond is staggered.</li><li>In a chair drawing, lines that should be parallel must be drawn parallel: ring bonds in three pairs, and each outward C-H parallel to the ring bonds one bond away.</li></ul>"
