        "tert-Butanol is the least acidic alcohol in solution but the most acidic in the gas phase. How can both be true?",
        "In solution the deciding factor is solvation: the bulky tert-butyl group keeps solvent molecules away from the charged oxygen, so tert-butoxide is the least stabilized anion. In the gas phase there is no solvent, and the larger alkyl group, which can spread the charge within the ion, gives the most stable anion. Acidity depends on the surroundings as well as on the molecule."
