    "h": "In short",
    "qs": [],
    "html": "<ul><li>Amines, alcohols, ethers, thiols and sulfides have lone pairs and are Lewis bases, and an ammonium ion is the conjugate acid of an amine.</li><li>Basicity increases to the left across a row and going up a column: HS− < HO− < H2N− < H3C−, and the acids H2S, H2O, NH3, CH4 run the opposite way.</li><li>pKa = −log Ka, and a lower pKa means a stronger acid: HI −10, HBr −9, HCl −8, H3O+ −1.5, CF3CO2H −1.5, HF 4, RCO2H 4, H2O 15.7, ROH about 16, HC≡CH 25, RNH2 38, alkane 50 to 60.</li><li>To explain acidity, compare the conjugate bases: induction, size, hybridization, aromaticity, resonance and electronegativity each stabilize a base.</li><li>CF3CO2H is a stronger acid than RCO2H by induction, acetylene (sp) is stronger than ethane (sp3) by hybridization, and H2S is stronger than H2O by size.</li><li>In solution a small alkoxide is better solvated and more stable, so methanol (15.5) is more acidic than tert-butanol (17.0), and the order reverses in the gas phase.</li><li>An acid-base equilibrium lies on the side of the weaker acid, the one with the higher pKa.</li><li>The methyl anion is a very strong base (conjugate acid pKa 50 to 60), and methyllithium behaves like it.</li></ul>"
