    "h": "Making a Grignard reagent: R-X and magnesium in diethyl ether",
    "html": "<p>The second kind of organometallic reagent is the <b>Grignard reagent, R-Mg-X</b>, where X is a halogen. The way to think about it is written beside the formula: <b>R:<sup>−</sup> (MgX)<sup>+</sup> → a strong base</b>. Once again the carbon carries the electron pair.</p>\n<p>The recipe:</p>\n<p><b>R-X + {{Mg}}<sup>0</sup> in diethyl ether → R-MgX</b></p>\n<p>For example, with 2-bromopropane:</p>\n[[rxn:CC(C)Br>Mg;diethyl ether>CC(C)[Mg]Br]]\n<p>The magnesium atom ends up <b>between</b> the carbon and the halogen. Nothing is lost: every atom of the halide and the magnesium atom are in the product.</p>\n<p>The two preparations side by side:</p>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Organolithium</th><th>Grignard</th></tr><tr><td>Formula</td><td>R-Li</td><td>R-Mg-X</td></tr><tr><td>Made from</td><td>R-Br or R-Cl</td><td>R-X (X = halogen)</td></tr><tr><td>Metal</td><td>{{Li}}<sup>0</sup>, 2 equivalents</td><td>{{Mg}}<sup>0</sup></td></tr><tr><td>Solvent</td><td>pentane</td><td>diethyl ether</td></tr><tr><td>Other product</td><td>LiBr (or LiCl)</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>Think of it as</td><td>{{R-}} with {{Li+}}</td><td>R:<sup>−</sup> with (MgX)<sup>+</sup></td></tr></table>\n<p>For the reactions in this course the two reagents do the same job. The 11/03 lecture says so directly: <b>RLi and RMgX both act as R:<sup>−</sup></b>.</p>\n<details class=\"rc-more rc-micro\"><summary>Closer look: Why one magnesium is enough</summary><div class=\"rc-body\"><p>Magnesium is in the second column of the periodic table and has two valence electrons, where lithium has one. The same two electrons are needed here as in the lithium recipe, and a single magnesium atom can supply both, becoming {{Mg^2+}}. Because one metal ion now carries two positive charges, it holds both of the negative partners, the carbon group and the halide, which is why the formula is written R-Mg-X with magnesium in the middle and why no separate salt is formed.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"rc-more rc-micro\"><summary>Closer look: What the ether does</summary><div class=\"rc-body\"><p>Diethyl ether is chosen for two reasons that come straight from the acids lecture. First, like pentane, it has no acidic hydrogen: only C-H bonds, so the reagent does not destroy it. Second, ether is a Lewis base. The lone pairs on its oxygen are donated to the magnesium, which is electron poor, and this keeps the reagent stable and dissolved. An alcohol would also have lone pairs on oxygen, but its O-H hydrogen ({{pKa}} about 16) would be removed at once by the carbanion. An ether has the lone pairs without the acidic hydrogen.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"rc-more rc-macro\"><summary>Bigger picture: The reagent of Question 1D</summary><div class=\"rc-body\"><p>The reagent is named after Victor Grignard, who received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1912 for it, and it remains one of the standard ways to make carbon-carbon bonds, including in the manufacture of medicines. On the past exam it appears in the highest-valued single part, Question 1D (55 points): phenylmagnesium bromide, PhMgBr, reacts with carbon dioxide and then acid. The first thing to write for that question is the idea from this section: PhMgBr behaves as Ph:<sup>−</sup> with (MgBr)<sup>+</sup>.</p></div></details>\n<p class=\"rc-src\">Original notes: <a class=\"rc-page\" href=\"../archive/pages/L1029-5.png\">10/29 page 5</a>.</p>",
