    "html": "<div class=\"rc-hw\"><b>The professor's prompt.</b> HW: (1-bromoethyl)cyclobutane + ethanol gives ? Predict the {{SN1}} product. Predict the {{E1}} product.</div>\n<p class=\"rc-src\">Original notes: <a class=\"rc-page\" href=\"../archive/pages/L1110-5.png\">11/10 page 5</a>.</p>\n<details class=\"rc-more rc-steps\"><summary>Try it on paper first. Then open the worked steps</summary><div class=\"rc-body\"><ol><li><b>Classify the conditions.</b> The carbon bearing Br has two carbons attached (the {{CH3}} and a ring carbon): secondary. Ethanol is a polar protic solvent, a poor nucleophile and a weak base. There is no strong base or good nucleophile. That is the {{SN1}} / {{E1}} box. [[mol:CC(Br)C1CCC1]]</li><li><b>Ionize.</b> The C-Br bond breaks (rate-determining) to give a secondary carbocation next to the ring, and {{Br-}}. [[mol:C[CH+]C1CCC1]]</li><li><b>SN1 without rearrangement.</b> Ethanol attacks the cationic carbon with an oxygen lone pair. A second ethanol removes the O-H proton. The product is the ethyl ether, (1-ethoxyethyl)cyclobutane, formed as a racemic mixture because the cation is planar. [[mol:CCOC(C)C1CCC1]]</li><li><b>E1 without rearrangement.</b> Ethanol takes a hydrogen from a carbon next to the cation. Taking the ring C-H gives the more substituted alkene, ethylidenecyclobutane (three carbon groups on the double bond). Taking a {{CH3}} hydrogen gives vinylcyclobutane, the less substituted, minor alkene. [[mol:CC=C1CCC1]] [[mol:C=CC1CCC1]]</li><li><b>The rearrangement.</b> The cation is next to a strained four-membered ring. A ring C-C bond shifts to the cationic carbon (arrow from that ring bond to {{C+}}). The ring expands to five members, which relieves ring strain, and the product is the 2-methylcyclopentyl cation. [[rxn:C[CH+]C1CCC1>>CC1CCC[CH+]1]]</li><li><b>Products after ring expansion.</b> Ethanol attacks the new cation: 1-ethoxy-2-methylcyclopentane ({{SN1}}). Loss of a neighbouring hydrogen gives 1-methylcyclopentene, the more substituted alkene ({{E1}}). The ring-expanded cation can also take a hydride shift to become the tertiary 1-methylcyclopentyl cation, which leads to 1-ethoxy-1-methylcyclopentane and the same 1-methylcyclopentene. [[mol:CCOC1CCCC1C]] [[mol:CC1=CCCC1]]</li></ol><p><b>Answer.</b> Direct {{SN1}} product: (1-ethoxyethyl)cyclobutane, racemic. Direct {{E1}} product: ethylidenecyclobutane (major alkene), with some vinylcyclobutane. Because the secondary cation sits next to a four-membered ring, it can also rearrange by ring expansion to a cyclopentyl cation, giving 1-ethoxy-2-methylcyclopentane ({{SN1}}) and 1-methylcyclopentene ({{E1}}).</p></div></details>"
