        "Why does the base remove a hydrogen from the β carbon and not from the α carbon?",
        "The electrons from the broken C-H bond need somewhere to go. On a β carbon they can slide over to form a π bond to the α carbon as the leaving group departs. A hydrogen on the α carbon itself has no such outlet, since its electrons cannot form a double bond to the same carbon."
