    "h": "In short",
    "qs": [],
    "html": "<ul><li>Collect four facts in order: the class of the carbon bearing the leaving group, the reagent (base strength, nucleophilicity, bulk), the solvent, and the temperature.</li><li>Methyl is always {{SN2}}: no carbocation can form and there is no β hydrogen.</li><li>Primary is {{SN2}} with good nucleophiles and with small strong bases, and {{E2}} only with a bulky strong base.</li><li>Secondary with a strong base is {{E2}} in any solvent.</li><li>Secondary with a weak base is {{SN1}}/{{E1}} in a polar protic solvent and {{SN2}} in a polar aprotic one.</li><li>Tertiary never does {{SN2}}: a strong base gives {{E2}}, and otherwise the carbocation forms and gives {{SN1}}/{{E1}}.</li><li>After choosing, apply the other issues: anti-periplanar geometry, Zaitsev or Hofmann, inversion or racemization, rearrangement, and heat favoring elimination.</li><li>The flowchart is a guide, and the handout says there will be exceptions.</li></ul>"
