        "Why does swapping which partner is the alkoxide and which is the halide change the outcome completely?",
        "The alkoxide is both a nucleophile and a strong base, and the halide's structure decides which role it plays. Facing a methyl or primary carbon it can reach, it substitutes. Facing a tertiary or hindered secondary carbon it cannot reach, it removes a β hydrogen and gives an alkene."
