        "Why does the alkoxide formed in step 1 attack the neighbouring carbon so readily?",
        "It is a strong nucleophile held right next to a carbon with a good leaving group, so it does not have to find a partner by collision. It is already in position for backside attack. Chloride is a weak base and leaves easily, which more than makes up for forming a strained ring."
