    "h": "In short",
    "qs": [],
    "html": "<ul><li>To make an ether by {{SN2}}, use a methyl or primary halide and put the bulky group in the alkoxide; a tertiary or secondary halide with an alkoxide gives {{E2}}.</li><li>An {{SN2}} nucleophile and its electrophile can be in the same molecule, and then the product is a ring, as when an alkoxide closes an epoxide.</li><li>Strained epoxides open by {{SN2}} at the less hindered carbon, even though the leaving group is an alkoxide.</li><li>A reagent with two nucleophilic ends and one with two leaving groups can react twice, giving a ring or an open chain depending on the partner for the second step.</li><li>On a cyclohexane, {{E2}} needs the leaving group axial and an axial hydrogen on the next carbon, and this geometry overrules the Zaitsev rule.</li><li>Two eliminations from a dibromide can give an alkyne, a conjugated diene or an allene, so list every β hydrogen at each stage.</li><li>A carbocation next to a four-membered ring rearranges by ring expansion, and an OH on the carbon that becomes positive leads to a ketone.</li><li>By the n + 1 rule a septet is a hydrogen with six equivalent neighbours: the CH of an isopropyl group.</li></ul>"
